WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
If you're building agents that interact with real users, ambiguous goal specification is unavoidable.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
We'll spend the next few minutes going over the two core strategies for dealing with unclear instructions,

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
how iterative refinement loops close quality gaps,

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
and why documenting assumptions matters for human oversight and auditability.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
What should an agent do when the instruction it was handed can be read several different ways?

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
That's an ambiguous goal, a task specification that doesn't fully define the expected outcome,

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.000
and leaves the agent to pick an interpretation.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
The response is either clarify first or assume and proceed.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Clarify first means pausing execution and asking a human for more information,

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
which costs latency but protects you when a wrong reading would be expensive.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Assume and proceed means making a reasonable inference and continuing,

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
which is the right call for low stakes, reversible, or time-sensitive work.

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
The whole skill is knowing which response fits the situation in front of you,

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
and that's what we're building here.

16
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Some situations make assumption-based execution genuinely risky,

17
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
and those are the cases where clarifying up front is the right call.

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Irreversible actions are the clearest example.

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
If the agent is about to delete data, send an external communication,

20
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.000
or commit a transaction that can't be undone, a wrong assumption could cause significant harm.

21
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.000
High-cost operations are similar.

22
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.000
If a task triggers an expensive API call or a long batch process,

23
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
it's worth a brief delay to confirm the scope is right.

24
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Scope uncertainty is the third signal.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
When the agent doesn't know what's in bounds, wrong assumptions don't just cause minor errors.

26
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
They cause the agent to operate on entirely the wrong data or system.

27
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
On the flip side, clarifying every ambiguity makes agents slow, brittle, and frustrating to work with.

28
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The assume-and-proceed strategy is appropriate when the action is low stakes and easily reversible.

29
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
If the agent makes a wrong call, the user can undo it without much cost.

30
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Time-sensitive tasks are another case.

31
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Sometimes the window for action is narrow enough that waiting for clarification means missing the opportunity entirely.

32
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
And sometimes the context is clear enough that the right interpretation is obvious,

33
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.000
even if the instruction wasn't perfectly worded.

34
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The goal isn't maximum caution.

35
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.000
It's appropriate judgment based on actual risk,

36
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
and that judgment needs to be calibrated to the real costs involved.

37
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
It helps to put the two strategies directly side-by-side.

38
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Clarifying up front adds latency and requires the human to be available and responsive.

39
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
It's justified when getting it wrong would cause irreversible damage, trigger high costs,

40
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
or send the agent off into the wrong scope entirely.

41
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Assuming-and-proceeding maintains task velocity and doesn't require human involvement.

42
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
It's justified when the action is reversible.

43
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The stakes are low, and the context makes the intent reasonably clear.

44
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Neither strategy is universally better.

45
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.000
The right answer is always situational,

46
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
and agents that apply the same strategy to every ambiguous instruction

47
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000
will get it wrong in one direction or the other more often than they should.

48
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Three practical criteria help structure the clarify-versus-assume decision.

49
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Reversibility is probably the most important.

50
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.000
If the action can be undone easily and cheaply,

51
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
proceeding on an assumption carries much lower risk.

52
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Stake level is second.

53
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
High-cost or high-impact operations justify the latency of a clarification request

54
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.000
in ways that routine, low-cost tasks don't.

55
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Scope clarity is third.

56
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
When the boundaries of the task are genuinely ambiguous,

57
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
a wrong assumption doesn't just affect this one action.

58
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
It can cause the agent to work on the wrong data or the wrong system

59
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
for an extended sequence of steps before the error surfaces.

60
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Clear scope reduces that cascading risk significantly.

61
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.000
When a single pass isn't good enough,

62
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
iterative refinement is the pattern that closes the gap.

63
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
A generator agent produces a candidate output.

64
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
An evaluator agent, which might be a separate model or just a structured critique prompt,

65
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
scores that output against a rubric and returns specific feedback

66
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.000
rather than a bare pass or fail.

67
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.000
The generator takes that critique and produces a revised version.

68
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Convergence criteria decide when the loop stops.

69
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Either the quality bar is met or the maximum iteration count is reached.

70
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
That cap isn't optional.

71
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Without it, the loop can run indefinitely on tasks where the evaluator is hard to satisfy.

72
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.000
This is called the evaluator-optimizer loop,

73
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.000
and it earns its cost on work like writing and analysis,

74
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.000
where quality is hard to specify up front but easy to recognize.

75
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.000
When an agent proceeds on assumptions,

76
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.000
the best practice is to make those assumptions explicit in the output or the execution log.

77
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.000
This isn't just a courtesy.

78
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
It's a meaningful audit capability.

79
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
A human reviewer can check whether the assumption was reasonable

80
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.000
without rerunning the full task from scratch.

81
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Well-documented assumptions reduce audit burden, speed up review cycles,

82
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.000
and make the agent's reasoning transparent enough to catch problems before they compound.

83
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.000
The longer-term solution is to improve goal specification design at the source.

84
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Precise, well-scoped instructions reduce the number of assumptions agents need to make in the first place.

85
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
That's a design investment that pays off across every task that touches that specification.

86
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.000
It's fun to treat vague instructions as a challenge for how reliable your system is.

87
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
If something is cheap to undo, the agent should just move forward.

88
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.000
If something is expensive or permanent, it should stop and ask permission.

89
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.000
That single question carries most of the judgment here.

90
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.000
It only holds up if the agent writes down what it assumed

91
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
because a reviewer can't check reasoning that was never communicated.

92
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.000
And that's it for this lesson.

93
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.000
I'll see you in the next one.

